usage: janus dead-letters replay [-h] [--environment ENVIRONMENT]
                                 [--project-root PROJECT_ROOT]
                                 --source-id SOURCE_ID (--item-key KEY |
                                 --all) --reason REASON [--execute]
                                 [--include-disabled]

Show what a release followed by a resuming run would do, and, with --execute,
do it.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --environment ENVIRONMENT
                        Environment profile name under conf/environments
                        without the .yaml suffix.
  --project-root PROJECT_ROOT
                        Project root used to resolve conf/ and data/ paths.
  --source-id SOURCE_ID
                        Configured source_id whose dead letters to read;
                        disabled sources included.
  --item-key KEY        A recorded item key, as `list` prints it; repeat for
                        several. Quote a key that holds spaces; join one that
                        begins with '-' to the option with '='.
  --all                 Every recorded entry. Releasing them all is asked for
                        by name, never by default.
  --reason REASON       Why they are released. With --execute it is recorded
                        in the history file with the operator
                        ($JANUS_OPERATOR, else $USER). Required: every state
                        change is recorded.
  --execute             Release, then run the source with --execute --resume.
                        Without it, nothing is written.
  --include-disabled    Allow --execute to run a source that is configured but
                        disabled.

Without --execute this is a dry run: nothing is released, nothing runs and
nothing is written. It prints what a resuming run would retry, what stays
skipped and where the run picks up. With --execute the entries are released,
the history record noting the replay, and the source runs as `janus run` runs
it with --execute and --resume. A release followed by that run is the same
operation in two steps.
